    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a> held that a society’s mode of production is the base of its politics, law and ideas, and that class struggle drives historical change.</li><li>Marxism divided into many schools after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>’s death; the most prominent in the 20th century was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>, the official ideology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and other Marxist states, while Western Marxism, libertarian Marxism and Marxist humanism often criticised state socialism.</li><li>Critics from several quarters, among them Karl Popper, Austrian economists and anarchists, attacked its predictions, its economics and its view of the state, while its concepts of alienation, exploitation and class struggle entered the social sciences and humanities.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a> did not favour the word Marxism, and in 1882 he reported that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> had said of the views of Paul Lafargue, a self-proclaimed Marxist, that if they were Marxist, then “one thing is certain and that is that I am not a Marxist.”</p></aside>
    </section>

