    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1845</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> sets out the materialist conception of history in The German Ideology</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a> publish the 12,000-word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">Communist Manifesto</a> in February</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1884</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a> publishes Origins of the Family, Private Property, and the State after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>’s death in 1883</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1917</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> take power from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-provisional-government">Russian Provisional Government</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1932</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>’s Economic and Philosophic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscripts</a> of 1844 appear, the birth of Marxist humanism</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1949</span><span class="rf-text">The People’s Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> is declared after the Chinese Communist Revolution</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1959</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-revolution">Cuban Revolution</a> brings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a> to power; he later adopts the Leninist model</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1991</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> dissolves, and its constituent nations abandon Marxist–Leninist models</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-marx.jpg" alt="Karl Marx" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Marx_001_restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Jabez Edwin Mayall, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Marxism originated in the works of the 19th-century German philosophers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a>. They first met in September 1844 and, finding that they held similar views of philosophy and socialism, collaborated on works such as Die heilige Familie (The Holy Family). After Marx was deported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in January 1845 they moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, which permitted greater freedom of expression than other European countries, and in January 1846 they established the Communist Correspondence Committee in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>.</p><p>Marx gave his account of the materialist conception of history in The German Ideology (1845) and in the preface to A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy (1859). In 1847 the two began writing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a>, based on Engels’s The Principles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">Communism</a>, and published the 12,000-word pamphlet in February 1848. Belgium expelled them in March, and they moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, where they published the radical newspaper Neue Rheinische Zeitung.</p><p>After Marx died in 1883, Engels became the editor and translator of his writings. The word Marxism was popularised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky">Karl Kautsky</a>, who considered himself an orthodox Marxist in the dispute between Marx’s orthodox and revisionist followers; his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-bernstein">Eduard Bernstein</a> later adopted it too. Engels did not support the term, which he held was used as a rhetorical qualifier by those casting themselves as genuine followers of Marx.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Marxism uses a materialist methodology, later known as historical materialism, to analyse social change from the collective ways in which humans make their living. A society’s mode of production forms its economic base, and from it rise legal and political forms and a dominant ideology, the superstructure. The relation is reflexive: the superstructure acts back on the base, so that the relationship is dialectic, driven by conflicts and contradictions. When the forces of production, such as technology, outgrow the existing relations of production, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> wrote, an era of social revolution begins. He described four stages of development in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>: primitive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, slave society, feudalism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>.</p><p>Under capitalism the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>, who own the means of production, face the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a>, who must sell their labour power. In Marx’s theory, exploitation takes the form of surplus value, the difference between the value a worker produces and the wage received, and the worker’s entry into employment is voluntary only in appearance, since the worker must work or starve. Alienation, the estrangement of people from their humanity, is in his view a systematic result of capitalism.</p><p>Marxists expect the state, seen as a weapon of one class over another, to be seized by working-class revolution and eventually to wither away, leaving a classless society. Marx himself used socialism and communism interchangeably; the distinction as two stages entered Marxism after his death.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg.jpg" alt="Rosa Luxemburg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rosa_Luxemburg_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>’s death his ideas have been elaborated and adapted by many thinkers and movements, so that there is no single, definitive Marxist theory. The failure of the 1905 Russian Revolution, and of socialist movements to resist the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, led to renewed theoretical work by Lenin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a> on Marx’s crisis theory and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky">Kautsky</a> and Bernstein stood on opposite sides of the dispute between orthodox and revisionist followers.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> of 1917 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> founded the first socialist state, and in 1919 the Soviet government set up the Communist Academy and the Marx–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a>–Lenin Institute for doctrinal study. Lenin’s death in 1924 opened a struggle between Stalin and Trotsky over the interpretation of Marxist and Leninist theory, and this period is marked by the development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a> as the dominant strain. Marxist humanism was born in 1932 with the publication of Marx’s early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and Western Marxism was accepted within Western academia in the 1940s.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the Communist Party, founded in 1921, took power in 1949, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> developed a Marxism for China built on the peasantry, New Democracy, the mass line and people’s war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-revolution">Cuban Revolution</a> followed in 1959. Further schools include Austro-Marxism, council <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurocommunism">Eurocommunism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trotskyism">Trotskyism</a>, Luxemburgism, Marxist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feminism">feminism</a> and libertarian Marxism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping.jpg" alt="Deng Xiaoping" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deng_Xiaoping_at_the_arrival_ceremony_for_the_Vice_Premier_of_China_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> of 1917 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> took power, founding the first socialist state. They promised to end Russian involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and Lenin’s government instituted universal education, universal healthcare and expanded women’s rights. After the revolution the Soviet government fought the White Movement and several independence movements in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>.</p><p>After Lenin’s death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a> became the official ideology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and of other Marxist states. Under Stalin, Soviet archaeology was reformed on Marxist lines and criticised as inherently bourgeois, and Marxist historiography suffered as the government demanded overdetermined historical writing; the History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a> (Bolsheviks), published in the 1930s, was written to justify the nature of Bolshevik party life under Stalin.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the People’s Republic was declared in 1949, and from 1966 to 1976 the Maoist government carried out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> to purge Chinese society of capitalist elements. After Mao’s death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> revised or abandoned many of its policies and encouraged privatised industry. Castro adopted the Leninist model in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and allied with the Soviet Union. In the late 1980s and early 1990s most of the states that had professed Marxism–Leninism collapsed: Gorbachev’s reforms, together with rising ethnic nationalism, led to the Soviet Union’s dissolution in late 1991, and its successor nations abandoned Marxist–Leninist models, most converting to capitalist economies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin.jpg" alt="Mikhail Bakunin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Mikhail Bakunin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mikhail_Bakunin_Nader_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nadar, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Criticism has come from many ideologies and disciplines: alleged lack of internal consistency, historical determinism, the suppression of individual rights, difficulties in implementing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, and economic problems such as distorted or absent price signals and reduced incentives. Karl Popper held that Marxism was at first scientific because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> made predictions, but that when they were not borne out the theory avoided falsification through ad hoc hypotheses and degenerated into pseudoscientific dogma. Leszek Kołakowski wrote that Marx’s theory was incomplete or ambiguous in many places and could be applied in contradictory ways, and judged some laws of dialectics to be truisms, dogmas or nonsense.</p><p>Critics also point to the absence of worsening crises that overthrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> and to revolutions occurring in undeveloped regions rather than the most advanced capitalist nations. The claim that Marxist states had lower living standards than capitalist ones has been disputed. Dmitriev and Bortkiewicz alleged internal inconsistency in Marx’s value theory, and Okishio’s theorem casts doubt on the falling rate of profit; Austrian economists such as Böhm-Bawerk and Mises attacked the law of value and the possibility of socialist economic calculation.</p><p>Anarchists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Mikhail Bakunin</a> criticised Marx’s authoritarian bent, and libertarian and classical liberal economists argue that the lack of free markets leads to authoritarianism. Among Marxists, Alex Callinicos accused Western Marxists of making conceptual clarification an end in itself.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/max-weber"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/max-weber.jpg" alt="Max Weber" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/max-weber">Max Weber</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Max_Weber,_1918.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ernst Gottmann, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Marxism stands as one of the most influential and controversial intellectual traditions in modern history. It has inspired revolutions, social movements and parties across the world, and concepts such as alienation, exploitation and class struggle have become part of the social sciences and humanities. It has shaped fields including anthropology, archaeology, economics, historiography, literary criticism, sociology, education and political science. A 2007 survey of American professors by Neil Gross and Solon Simmons found that 17.6% of social science professors and 5.0% of humanities professors identified as Marxists.</p><p>Marxian economics, though considered heterodox, has contributed to mainstream understanding of the global economy, and some concepts, such as creative destruction, have been fitted for use in capitalist systems. British Marxist historians such as Christopher Hill, E. P. Thompson and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-hobsbawm">Eric Hobsbawm</a> developed history from below, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> School combined some Marxist concepts with the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-weber">Max Weber</a>. Because of its former state-supported position, Marxist thought met a backlash in post-communist states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, while it remains prominent in sociological research sanctioned by Communist-ruled states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>At the turn of the 21st century China, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> remained the only officially Marxist–Leninist states. Marxist theory has continued to develop in the work of David Harvey, Nancy Fraser, Erik Olin Wright, Silvia Federici and others.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Marxism is a materialist philosophy and method of analysis that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> developed in 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, centred on class struggle and the critique of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>. It split into many schools, the most prominent being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>, and it shaped revolutions, states and academic disciplines.</em></p>

